#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286" :question/problem "![](images/00/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916 :topic/math-academy-id 3711 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions Over General Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06952f7f-4bfc-548a-858e-98687ea45c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324032} {:answer/id #uuid "5f81d6d6-6127-530e-ac7f-c4e213abe7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324769} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f6ee80-a912-57f5-a285-2e0f6930700b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325354} {:answer/id #uuid "aa85942c-1e5b-56e8-8e97-ecf540eb889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325422} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a60610-258b-5f05-a158-e2b14597409d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f81d6d6-6127-530e-ac7f-c4e213abe7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324769} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a311ac2-5c19-587c-a392-8c71de68fc0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eca1e45a-1d3f-5ae4-a5ee-83a793b7f6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39342" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad37157-9a52-53de-8a88-f35bbd20faae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324312} {:answer/id #uuid "41eaf9b6-c62c-5a18-b922-6e8c121cab26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324503} {:answer/id #uuid "66de0861-eaa6-53b8-b659-704734a18a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324831} {:answer/id #uuid "8772142e-f3d2-5968-9d7e-fa44127516d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325124} {:answer/id #uuid "bc84b5b2-8cab-547c-8cee-e174ba0ddddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41eaf9b6-c62c-5a18-b922-6e8c121cab26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324503} :answer-field/id #uuid "813be4d0-fa70-524e-afa9-65eb377f0d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e395277-17c1-5003-9ac0-10817fce6c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-39674" :question/problem "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6cd156-8ca4-5855-a3cc-5f0d395f4aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324341} {:answer/id #uuid "317a4675-7255-5700-9ad9-8cfade1093ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324370} {:answer/id #uuid "68507ca4-abf8-5cd6-8193-9965c1c024dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324842} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a34229-1bb6-551f-bdc6-3c2c3f4134b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325504} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeccb5b-f4c0-5768-a9a9-bdc8a37556ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a34229-1bb6-551f-bdc6-3c2c3f4134b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325504} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d160d2-e817-535b-93be-44d3599c92c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c31eda49-a231-50f2-81e8-83c448690ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39673" :question/problem "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\,i+(t+(1+t))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =((-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f99a0c2-ff39-555f-979d-c5dceb67d68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342761} {:answer/id #uuid "561cf640-b717-5a0c-a5a7-05b94a807500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342954} {:answer/id #uuid "b645e1eb-5ce9-56ca-8d1b-c4fb928eafdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343836} {:answer/id #uuid "d687ba62-fd77-5fb2-85a6-257e3f469e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344120} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5dfda-1ddb-554b-acda-daf0da60f92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f99a0c2-ff39-555f-979d-c5dceb67d68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342761} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab12311-2d02-5230-960f-45a14af856ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344760}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "616650f8-c315-5a80-80b8-1d1e65ec51b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-77180" :question/problem "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $B$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(0-b) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+t(⟨0,0⟩-⟨1,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨1-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=1-t$ and $y=1-t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2(1-t)+(1-t))\\,i+((1-t)-2(1-t))\\,j \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,i+(-1+t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =((3-3t)\\,i+(-1+t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i-j) \\\\  & =-3+3t+1-t \\\\  & =-2+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+(-2t+{t}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+(1-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21279c87-66bb-50e4-8d0a-b4f4a20df582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342511} {:answer/id #uuid "56b72719-148e-5b84-8585-452f45bb273d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342960} {:answer/id #uuid "5c203805-aaee-5456-a95d-7a12caeeb4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343009} {:answer/id #uuid "683b017e-1f61-5d0d-8485-fec00b137cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343128} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4af7cc-9165-5222-ac1c-d3e9cde8c428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21279c87-66bb-50e4-8d0a-b4f4a20df582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342511} :answer-field/id #uuid "22e376b5-f6bc-5044-8715-29e828c6d70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344556}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2144499-6ba0-5eff-ada5-d16752f7b523" :question/math-academy-id "q-77182" :question/problem "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i+(x-{y}^{2})\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the horizontal line segment from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =0+t(a-0) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,0⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =({x}^{2}+y)\\,i+(x-{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+0)\\,i+(t-{0}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)j \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc09296-8638-5bb9-aa09-d659533f54b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342737} {:answer/id #uuid "4f83c7c3-3dd2-503c-84e2-cff8e0497ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342899} {:answer/id #uuid "84da968a-d353-52cb-9a5b-8037954b3f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343377} {:answer/id #uuid "b500e96e-2b58-5d83-8ee1-056f5152c148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343823} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c60230-d8a1-5d13-ba1f-f00ed01733ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b500e96e-2b58-5d83-8ee1-056f5152c148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343823} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe814ee1-ffa1-535f-9662-cd673e89b2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60807a56-8605-5f52-976b-4c6cebda8f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77185" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the vertical line segment from $B$ to $D.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(d-b) \\\\  & =⟨-1,2⟩+t(⟨-1,0⟩-⟨-1,2⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-1,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =-\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=-1$ and $y=2-2t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x-y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(-1-(2-2t))\\,i+(2-2t-(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3+2t)\\,i+(3-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-2t)j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =((-3+2t)\\,i+(3-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(3-2t)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-6)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}+[2{t}^{2}-6t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}+[2({1}^{2})-6(1)-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccbd978-8163-5c7e-baae-b8ad95985a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342350} {:answer/id #uuid "453e49ec-25af-50ef-847c-f9e8f100fa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342807} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2918a-06f4-5e2f-8f95-8ce2e0a1d53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343281} {:answer/id #uuid "7c924cb2-9540-5923-aef1-54902d61b6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343312} {:answer/id #uuid "a953ff99-deba-55f2-9b54-5b5b5650e514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccbd978-8163-5c7e-baae-b8ad95985a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342350} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c5cc9a-ab7f-5bd2-8f67-b0bc3b91ae44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fc56ee5-fc5d-5429-bfdb-4254f7ee30fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-77191" :question/problem "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{3}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨-2,0⟩+t(⟨0,1⟩-⟨-2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-2+2t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(-2+2t)i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=-2+2t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j \\\\  & =((-2+2t)-2t)\\,i+(t-(-2+2t))\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =-2-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2-t)\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={[-2t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =[-2(1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0]-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "126d13a7-71fa-5051-a4a2-28f29e56cf03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415734} {:answer/id #uuid "2216ca5d-6c6a-5107-a068-323114e3fc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415735} {:answer/id #uuid "7aae6b46-36e9-538a-8e22-b5381b749d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415746} {:answer/id #uuid "7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415747} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea13c33-b0aa-51a8-a9cd-a56c81fb144a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415747} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adcd9af-5265-5496-ada0-3911ecad9cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415766}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3412530f-81b3-51bb-95a9-4599a55eb207" :question/math-academy-id "q-77250" :question/problem "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nLet ${C}_{2}$ be the section of parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from $A$ to $B.$ Then,\n $-{C}_{2}$ will be the same path but travelled from $B$ to $A.$ The path $-{C}_{2}$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+(4-{t}^{2})\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=4-{t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =x\\,i=t\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i-2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i)\\cdot (i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55fc80b9-6193-508c-b227-e04b2840c9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415741} {:answer/id #uuid "7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{112}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415745} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ded463-bc16-568f-8c0b-62a6554bc47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415756} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cf1e9e-5de9-575b-be80-c45bb969b3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff980988-6413-52f6-84a6-6d1daa6d7016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{112}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415745} :answer-field/id #uuid "b523b1e1-5cbd-5efb-b1af-a137b9b5cd25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8399ab15-70cf-5db8-b1d1-61088de736be" :question/math-academy-id "q-77244" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4ce481ac8babb7a3e5707f1ef44417b12e73abe4b4c2143c19b45332642c6d2.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment  from the point $B(0,8)$ to the point $A(-2,8),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from the point $A(-2,8)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from $A$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $-2\\le t\\le 0.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-{t}^{3}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =xy\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i-3{t}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (i-3{t}^{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{[-\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{6}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =16+[0-(-\\frac{(-{2}^{5})}{5}+\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{2})] \\\\  & =16-\\frac{192}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{112}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b97760-1703-5852-afc4-69acd21e7cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424603} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} {:answer/id #uuid "79d50006-6f06-56c3-bce2-b65f3e1eb21c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424607} {:answer/id #uuid "d37fe04f-4a91-5f51-9524-f7477c2a7f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424612} {:answer/id #uuid "e17522ae-4622-53a9-bb60-7bec4bbcce91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdba31b-0f4f-583e-a52e-f0510ad8e3c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424617}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "515517e3-98f2-5a9f-ba03-8d3b47263ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77258" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2})\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319" :question/problem "![](images/38/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/math-academy-id 3708 :topic/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and 