{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615} :answer-field/id #uuid "d716774a-ff3b-5a8f-950b-7d77b8284d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424618}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77145" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853 :topic/math-academy-id 2108 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions Over Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666 :topic/math-academy-id 3705 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a436c1fe-9875-5164-ae87-aa8cc5576ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177778} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cf854b-fd8f-54e9-82b6-a3ed3d261ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)y^{2}dx - x^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177779} {:answer/id #uuid "075dc6a4-8af8-5cf3-ae6f-ca3c20cc6c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)x^{2}dx - y^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177780} {:answer/id #uuid "46551c98-a762-5158-93fb-2a0daa1f08f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)y^{2}dx + x^{2}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177781} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3538cb-9aa2-5a37-a8c8-64931e534447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(y^{2} + x^{2} + 5)dxdydz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177782}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e716d21b-fca4-5aab-a10e-a4a0863ac589" :answer-